{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 103, :comments ["<b>How sweet</b> — a foreign word, and its meaning is 'were sweetened.' For it is not from the root of מליץ and מליצה, since מליץ is like מקים and the mem is not a root letter; likewise מליצה is like מריבה and the mem is not of the root. Therefore נמלצו is a foreign word, for it is like נפתחו, נשמרו, נדברו, and the mem of נמלצו is a root letter like any of these: פתח, שמר — for a letter does not come after the nun of nif'al except from the root, פ״א פעל. Therefore נמלצו is a foreign word. Therefore it is to be interpreted as 'were sweetened,' and the sense will be doubled, according to standard usage. Thus the sage, may he live, interpreted for me, and the word 'from those who give me counsel' is lacking [in the interpretation], for they are in the force of the agent. [The verse is read as though מטעמי is implied.]"], :book :psalms, :voice :ibn-ezra, :lang :en, :chapter 119, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [["Ibn Ezra on Psalms -- Daat" "http://www.daat.ac.il/he-il/tanach/parshanut_hamikra/full-text/eben-ezra/tehilim"] ["Wikisource Mikraot Gedolot" "http://en.wikisource.org/wiki/Mikraot_Gedolot/"] ["Tanakh, Venice, 1617-1619" "https://dlib.rsl.ru/viewer/01007514045#?page=4"]], :timestamp "2026-07-09T19:40:42.982414189Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}